39	# Android Setup (`cli.py`)
40	
41	## Tested on
42	
43	- Pixel 9 running Android 16 API 36
44	- Magisk 30.4
45	- Frida 17.2.14
46	- 5ire with Gemini 2.5 Flash
47	
48	## Device Requirements
49	
50	- A rooted Android device
51	
52	  - Take a look at the process with [Magisk](https://github.com/topjohnwu/Magisk)
53	
54	  - You can use an emulator, but a physical device is cooler :3 also with the emulator the networking isn't immediately obvious to setup, you'll have to use adb port forwarding or a non-standard network bridge in whatever emulator you're using
55	
56	  - Unless you're dealing with sensitive data on the device just set Magisk to auto approve any superuser request
57	
58	- A Frida server
59	
60	  - Just use the latest [Frida Magisk module](https://github.com/ViRb3/magisk-frida/releases). Make sure to match the client version of the Frida you choose to use / compile
61	
62	- Install termux
63	
64	  - Use the [GitHub version of termux](https://github.com/termux/termux-app)
65	
66	- Inside of termux install the root repo 
67	
68	  - `pkg install root-repo`
69	
70	- Inside of termux install all the packages (I might be missing stuff here)
71	
72	  - The following should install necessary build tools: `pkg install git python clang gcc rust nodejs ninja pkg-config libresolv-wrapper wget` 
73	
74	  - Do NOT install frida with pip -- it will fail cuz of the C bindings.
75	
76	- Manually build the Frida client
77	
78	  - Find the latest frida-core-devkit for the appropriate architecture and use `wget` to grab the .xz file
79	
80	  - `mkdir -p frida-core && tar -xf <yourdevkit.tar.xz> -C frida-core`
81	
82	  - Grab the frida-python project `git clone https://github.com/frida/frida-python.git`
83	
84	- Run the following (there might be missing steps here)
85	
86	```
87	# This config should work but if it gives you trouble try hardcoding all the paths instead of using $HOME references
88	
89	export HOME=/data/data/com.termux/files/home
90	export PREFIX=/data/data/com.termux/files/usr
91	export FRIDA_CORE_DEVKIT="$HOME/frida-core"
92	export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$HOME/frida-core"
93	
94	# CHANGE THIS BASED ON WHAT ARCHITECTURE YOU'RE ON
95	
96	cd $HOME/frida-python/deps/toolchain-linux-arm64/bin/
97	
98	# Some of the toolchain native binaries are broken so we'll swap them out here
99	
100	# Replace pkg-config
101	mv pkg-config pkg-config.backup
102	ln -sf $PREFIX/bin/pkg-config ./pkg-config
103	
104	# Replace ninja
105	mv ninja ninja.backup
106	ln -sf $PREFIX/bin/ninja ./ninja
107	
108	# In the termux environment libresolv doesn't exist but libresolv_wrapper does, so we'll make a symlink here too
109	
110	ln -sf $PREFIX/lib/libresolv_wrapper.so $PREFIX/lib/libresolv.so
111	
112	# Build with hijacked toolchain
113	
114	cd $HOME/frida-python
115	rm -rf build/
116	
117	LDFLAGS="-L$PREFIX/lib -lpython3.12 -llog -latomic" \
118	pip install . --no-index --no-build-isolation
119	
120	# LEAVE THE frida-python library before testing!
121	
122	cd ~
123	python3 -c "import frida;print(frida.__version__)
124	```
125	
126	- Setup termux environment
127	
128	  - Place `cli.py` in your home directory which should be `/data/data/com.termux/files/home` 
129	
130	  - Inside of the home folder `/data/data/com.termux/files/home`, install node dependencies `npm install buffer frida-java-bridge process`. This is needed for Frida 17 because you need to compile (in our case bundle and transpile) javascript code in order for it to run. This is due to the script's design of using temporary files inside of `/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/tmp` to handle bundling and transpiling. By hardcoding the path to home instead of using the default temp directory, we ensure the node_modules are persistent and reachable by esbuild. The process is: write temp file -> bundle/transpile -> pass "compiled" logic to the rest of the script.
131	
132	  - Install `esbuild` globally, this is what we'll use to bundle and transpile scripts instead of `frida-compile` because `frida-compile` uses V8's JIT compiler, which requires W^X memory — blocked by Android's `untrusted_app` SELinux domain in Termux (causes SIGSEGV). `esbuild` is a statically linked Go binary with no JIT, so it runs cleanly. Can install with `npm` but can also install via `pkg install esbuild`.
133	
134	- Install python dependencies
135	
136	  - `pip install -r requirements-android.txt`
137	
138	  - **WARNING** Do not run `pip install frida`, because it will break. Build from source per the instructions above.
139	
140	  - **WARNING** FastMCP 3.x has breaking API changes. This repo is still on FastMCP 2.x for both Android and iOS — migration hasn’t kept pace with upstream. Use `requirements-android.txt`; don’t install an unpinned `fastmcp`.
141	
142	- Install your target application. As a PoC we use [AndroGoat](https://github.com/satishpatnayak/AndroGoat)
143	
144	  - **NOTE**: For some devices, Magisk places the `su` binary at `/system_ext/bin/su`, which the default root detection in AndroGoat doesn't check. Fix by changing `if(isRooted())// || isRooted1())` --> `if(isRooted() || isRooted1())` or adding your su binary path to the `isRooted` array.
145	
